2 CONC n children and four &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;grandchildren&lt;/span&gt; against almost impossible conditions. Whole overcomin
2 CONC g the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;loss&lt;/span&gt; of several children from tuberculosis she remained a determined and &lt;/span
2 CONC &gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;devout&lt;/span&gt; servant of God. She raised their own food, slaughtered her own &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style
2 CONC =&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;animals&lt;/span&gt; and sewed all family clothing. Today, she is still remembered as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-siz
2 CONC e: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;Mrs. Robinson, &amp;ldquo;the Indian Lady on Russian Hill&amp;rdquo; in Hartshorne, Oklahoma. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&l
2 CONC t;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;In our family, Jane Impson Robinson is our &amp;ldquo;Famous Person&amp;rdquo;, a symbol of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;strength&lt;/span&gt; and spiritual leadership like so many original Choctaw enrollees. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span styl
2 CONC e=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;John W. Robinson, the son of N.C. and Jane Robinson was born in Kentucky &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; the year 1844. He moved to Indian Territory where he was a teacher and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;accomplished&lt;/span&gt; stonemason. He was at one time a teacher in a Choctaw Indian &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&am
