2 CONC ed States, Case No. 30, Gist vs. Robinette. (x) Bourbon Co., KY Deeds, L:477-483.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;TIMELINE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</
2 CONC line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1a) 1752, fall: Christopher GIST started the settlement for the Ohio Company between the Youghogania and Monongahela Rivers. He had agreed when he was employed by the Com
2 CONC pany to remove 150 or more families to the Ohio Company&apos;s land. The Company agreed that all families should receive 100 acres for each person up to four, and 50 acres for each person in excess of four. Within three years after settlement eac
2 CONC h settler was to pay the Company 4 pounds sterling for every 100 acres and the lands were to be free of quit rents for the first 5 years. Christopher GIST and his sons Richard, Nathaniel, and Thomas, his wife, and daughter Anne &quot;Nancy&quot; an
2 CONC d his daughter Violetta and her husband William CROMWELL were the first settlers. Thomas and his sister Anne lived on the plantation known as Mount Braddock, a few miles north of Uniontown, Fayette Co., PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1b,i) 1755, 9 Jul: When Gen. BRADDOCK marched from Fort Cumberland on his expedition to Fort Duquesne, Christopher GIST was selected as his head guide. GIST&apos;s son Nathanie
2 CONC l was employed as a scout, and his son Thomas as an assistant. When the General and his army crossed the Monongahela River, they were surprised by the French and Indians, who had been waiting for them. The general was wounded and his army cut to pi
2 CONC eces. A hasty retreat was made to GIST&apos;s plantation and then eastward. BRADDOCK died near the Great Meadows.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1i) 1758, Apr: He wa
2 CONC s with WASHINGTON&apos;s troops on the march of Ft. Duquesne and was employed by the Colonel to carry the regiment&apos;s pay from Williamsburg to Fort Loudoun.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 1
2 CONC 4pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1i) [1758]: Given an ensign&apos;s commission in Capt. Robert STEWART&apos;s Company.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1g) 1758, 9 Sep: He was a
2 CONC t Ligioner, PA with Maj. GRANT preparing to march to Fort Duquesne.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1g) 1758, 14 Sep: He took part in the battle on Grant&apos;s Hill, w
2 CONC as fired upon by the Indians, and was short through the right hand. After running some distance, he was obliged to surrender and was marched toward the fort.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt
2 CONC ;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1g) Was taken to the Wyandott Town on the straits Le Detroit, where he was adopted by the Indians. (He wrote a&lt;span class=&quot;Apple-converted-space&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&amp;ldquo;captivity journal.&amp;rdquo
2 CONC ; &lt;a href=&quot;https://journals.psu.edu/pmhb/article/view/41177/40898&quot;&gt;View of Thomas Gist&apos;s Indian Captivity 1758-1759 | Pennsylvania Magazine of History and Biography &lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br
2 CONC &gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1g) 1759, 12 Sep: He and two other prisoners escaped and made their way back home. Upon his return, he sent a letter to Gen. Thomas GAGE, Commander of the British Forces in America, tell
2 CONC ing of his capture, the strength of the French Army and the Nations of Indians near Detroit, and his escape from the Indians.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1k) 1760
2 CONC : He joined a VA Regiment and served as lieutenant.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1k) 1761: When the VA General Assembly passed an act for giving recompense to the of
2 CONC ficers, he received a full year&apos;s pay.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1l) 1762, 3 Apr: Mr. Edward PENDLETON stated that the House of Burgesses had passed a Resolu
2 CONC tion that the sum of 100 pounds be paid by the public to Lt. Thomas GIST as a reward for his bravery as an officer in the later VA Regiment, and as a recompense for the wound he received in Col. GRANT&apos;s engagement, and the hardships he underwe
2 CONC nt during a year&apos;s captivity by the Indians.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1m) 1763, 21 May: Thomas GIST presented a petition to the House of Burgesses of VA, st
2 CONC ating that he entered the service of the Colony in 1756 as an officer in the Regiment, and continued until it was disbanded in 1762; that in Col. GRANT&apos;s engagement in 1758 near Pittsburgh he received a wound in his right hand which has almos
2 CONC t deprived him of its use; that this greatly hindered him in his means of livelihood, and he did not have a sufficient fortune to support himself and prayed the consideration of the house.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1m) 1763, 27 May: His petition was presented and deferred to Edward PENDLETON, George WASHINGTON, Richard BLAND and William MASON. A pension of 25 pounds a year and for life was allowed.&lt;/span
2 CONC &gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1c) He returned to the settlement made by his father in 1753 on a tract lying on the waters of the Yohogania. In time he took over all the lan
2 CONC d claimed by his brothers. He built a home which he called &quot;Mt. Braddock&quot; on his 433 1/2 acre tract adjoining his father&apos;s home plantation. He lived here with his sister Anne until he died.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1770, 13 Oct: George WASHINGTON stopped to see Thomas and Anne on his way to the Ohio River. He wrote in his diary, &quot;when we came down the Hill to the Plantation of Mr. T
2 CONC hos. GIST, the Ld. appearing charming; that which lay level being as rich and black as anything could possibly be.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1770, 25 Nov: O
2 CONC n his return from the OH trip, George WASHINGTON wrote that he dined at Mr. Thos. GISTs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1p) 1771: He was appointed one of the Justice
2 CONC s of Bedford Co., PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1e) 1771, 13 Aug: Richard GIST of Bedford Co., PA deeded to Thomas GIST of the same place, for L50, all that trac
2 CONC t of land being on the Monongahela or Youghyana or both of them located in the Land Office of Philadelphia on 3 Apr 1769, surveyed and returned to the Office containing 318 acres of land. Recorded 31 Sep 1795, Fayette Co., PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1772, 25 Sep: Visited George WASHINGTON at Mt. Vernon. WASHINGTON wrote in his diary that &quot;Tom GIST came this aftern.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</
2 CONC line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1772, 26 Sep: WASHINGTON wrote in his diary, &quot;Mr. GIST and Mr. Val CRAWFORD both went away this morning.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p
2 CONC &gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1t) 1772, Nov: Thomas GIST made his will, mentioning that he was going on a long trip. &quot;I will and require that my lands on Mingo Creek and the waters of Shirtee may be so
2 CONC ld in order to pay off my just debts, and in case that should be found insufficient... I will that my executors shall also sell ... a tract of land adjoining the plantation on which I now live, it being the land I bought of my brother Richard GIST.
2 CONC .. I will unto my loving sister Ann GIST the one-half of my whole estate real and personal, but as my negro man Jason was given me by my father, and I would not have him sold, I do desire that he may be appraised and my said sister Ann to take hi
2 CONC m in her half part at the appraisment. I make her a present of half a dozen silver spoons which was also my Father&apos;s. I do will unto my loving daughter, Elizabeth JOHNSON, the other one-half part of my whole estate, real and personal, the sai
2 CONC d silver spoons excepted, and in case my sister Ann GIST should die without heirs... I will her half part to my daughter, Elizabeth JOHNSON, except the negro Jason and the silver spoons, which I give unto my brother, Nathaniel GIST, or his heirs ..
2 CONC ., and in case he and his heirs should die before her, I give the said negro and spoons to brother Richard GIST or his heirs. And in case he and his heirs should die before my sister Ann, then to my said daughter Elizabeth JOHNSON, and in case my s
2 CONC aid daughter should die before she comes of age or without heirs, I will her whole part to my said sister, Ann GIST, and in case she dies without heirs, I will my whole estate to be fivided between my two brothers, two-thirds to my brother Nathanie
2 CONC l GIST or his heirs, and the other one-third to my brother Richard GIST, and in case all the persons above mentioned should die without heirs, then I will my whole estate to be divided between my sister Violet CROMWELL&apos;s four children or thei
2 CONC r heirs. I do also will that my daughter Elizabeth JOHNSON be brought up in the persuasion of the church of England, genteel and with a reasonable education, and my sister Ann have the profit or income of her half of the estate for her care until s
2 CONC he be of the age of fifteen years, and I do constitute and appoint Mordecai GIST of Baltimore, Mercht., and George DAWSON of Bedford County in the Province of Pennsylvania, yeoman, guardians to my daughter Elizabeth JOHNSON, and also executors of t
2 CONC his last will.&quot; Wits. Henry HOUGHLAND, Alexander McLEAN, George DAWSON.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1s) 1772, 16 Nov: Thomas GIST and others left on a trip dow
2 CONC n the Mississippi from Fort Pitt to New Orleans. He kept a journal describing the land along the river, the falls of the Ohio, trees, shrubs, etc. He tells of hunting turkeys, buffalo, and geese along the way, sometimes going 2 or 3 miles back fro
2 CONC m the shore.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1s) They arrived at Lake Ponchertrain and attempted to reach the gulf. They saw Indians and some white folks along the wa
2 CONC y and a few families along the river near New Orleans.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1s) 1773, 13 Jul: Returned from his trip.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1r) 1773-1786: Thomas GIST, single, paid taxes in Bedford Co., Westmoreland Co. and Fayette Co., PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot
2 CONC ;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1q) 1773, Oct: He presided over the first court held in Westmoreland Co., PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1774, Feb: He we
2 CONC nt to see George WASHINGTON.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1) Bounty lands were granted to him in KY for services in the French and Indian War, and he also received l
2 CONC and that was due him as son and heir of Christopher GIST.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1n) 1774, 10 Feb: Thomas GIST, subaltern and staff officer, applied to Gov. DU
2 CONC MORE of VA for a certificate of 2,000 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1d) 1775: A tract of 3,000 acres was surveyed for Thomas GIST, representative of Christophe
2 CONC r GIST, Fincastle Co., VA, lying on the north side of the Kentucky River and on Gist&apos;s Creek, due Capt. Christopher GIST for his services in the French and Indian War.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;
2 CONC font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1n) 1775, Jun: Land was surveyed on his application of 10 Feb 1774, lying in Kentucky Co., VA on Gist&apos;s Creek and the waters of the Ohio. The land was patented 5 Jan 1780.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1d) 1775, Jun: &amp;nbsp;Surveyed for Thomas GIST, representative of Christopher GIST, 3000 acres of land in Fincastle Co., VA, lying on the north side of the Kentucky Ri
2 CONC ver on Gist&apos;s Creek, now Bourbon Co,, KY.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1g) 1775, 15 Jul: Nicholas CRESWELL wrote in his journal, &quot;Left Redstone Fort and af
2 CONC ter losing myself several times got to Captain Thomas GISTs. Very kindly treated by Miss Nancy (Ann) GIST, an agreeable young woman who informs me that there has been two very severe engagements at Boston and great numbers killed on both sides.&quo
2 CONC t;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1n) 1780, 5 Jan: Was granted patent for land surveyed Jun 1775 and applied for 10 Feb 1774.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&
2 CONC gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1f) 1780, 8 Mar: A warrant to survey land for Thomas GIST, assignee of Richard GIST, who claimed rights to 357 acres and allowance of the settlement made by his father Christoph
2 CONC er GIST in 1753, was made by the Commissioners of the District of Yohagania, Monongahela and Ohio Countis for adjusting claims to unpatented lands in this district.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-siz
2 CONC e: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1o) 1784, 12 Sep: George WASHINGTON wrote in his diary that he dined at Mr. Thomas GISTs at the foot of Laurel Hill.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;(1e) 1785, Oct: Land was surveyed for Thomas GIST: 620 1/4 acres and the usual allowance of 6 percent, situated on Dunbars and Gists Runs, surveyed to Thomas GIST in consequence of a Warrant of Preemption for 1,000 acres, granted to Thoma
2 CONC s GIST in right of his father Christopher GIST on 8 Mar 1780, and surveyed 26 Oct 1785; 439 1/2 acres and allowance, part of the preemption aforseaid surveyed 20 Oct 1785; also 436 1/2 acres and allowance in right of his father on VA Certificate
2 CONC , 4 Feb 1780 and surveyed 10 Oct 1785; 433 1/2 acres and allowance on Gists Run on VA certificate of 4 Feb 1780 and surveyed 21 Oct 1785; also 357 acres and allowance to Thomas GIST as assignee of Richard GIST on VA certificate of 4 Feb 1780 on Gis
2 CONC ts Run surveyed 19 Oct 1785; and 458 3/4 acres and allowance in right of Nathaniel GIST on a VA certificate issued 4 Feb 1780, and surveyed 19 Oct 1785, making in the whole 2,745 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1t) 1786, 25 Dec: Nathaniel GIST of Buckingham Co., VA entered a Caveat against the probate of Thomas GIST&apos;s will, but Alexander McCLEAN, Register of Wills of Fayette Co., PA, testified that h
2 CONC e had seen Thomas GIST sign the will.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href=&quot;tel:(1)%201786-1796&quot;&gt;(1) 1786-1796&lt;/a&gt;: Nathaniel GIST and his siste
2 CONC r Anne GIST were engaged in settling the estate of their deceased brother Thomas GIST, who left large tracts of land in Fayette Co., PA and Bourbon Co., KY.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;
2 CONC &quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1t) 1787, 19 Mar: Will probated, Fayette Co., PA. Letters of Administration were granted to his sister Ann GIST, the executors not living in the state.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;
2 CONC font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1u) 1788, 20 Nov: In order to pay a debt of 9 pounds owed by Thomas GIST, deceased, to Rekina SPIERS and Benjamin FRY, the Sheriff of Westmoreland Co., PA seized the tract of 400 acres on which the deceases Thom
2 CONC as GIST had lived and sold it at public auction. Isaac MEASON made the highest bid and bought it for 30 pounds.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1v) 1789, Jan: Nathanie
2 CONC l GIST brought suit against his sister Anne GIST to recover a debt which Thomas GIST owned him.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1v) 1789, 28 Mar: Anne GIST paid 212 pou
2 CONC nds PA money in part of a judgment obtained in the Supreme Court of PA against Thomas GIST&apos;s estate in behalf of Nathaniel GIST.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1u
2 CONC ) 1789, spring: The sheriff of Fayette Co., PA recovered against Anne GIST, administratrix of the goods of Thomas GIST deceased the sum of 120 punds by selling a tract of 620 1/2 acres to Isaac MEASON for 31 pounds.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><li
2 CONC ne>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1u) 1789, 3 Oct: To satisfy a 75 pound debt due Phillip SHUTE, the sheriff sold a tract containing 433 1/2 acres, which Isaac MEASON bought for 134 pounds.&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1h) 1789, 5 Oct: Andrew McCOWN of Fayette Co., PA and his wife Elizabeth, daughter of Thomas GIST, sold to Joseph ROBINETTE of Bourbon Co., KY a tra
2 CONC ct of 2,000 acres that had been granted by patent 5 Jan 1780 to Thomas GIST, deceased, Bourbon Co., KY.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1h) 1791, 29 Sep: Anne GIST of B
2 CONC uckingham Co., VA, conveyed to her brother Nathaniel GIST of the same county, one half of a tract of land in Bourbon Co., KY, containing 2,000 acres on Gist&apos;s Creek and the waters of the Ohio, granted by patent 5 Jan 1780 to Thomas GIST, decea
2 CONC sed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1w) 1793, 6 Mar: Suit was brought by Nathaniel GIST, heir at law to his brother Thomas GIST, against Joseph ROBINETT in Bourbon Co.
2 CONC , KY, regarding Thomas GIST&apos;s land in KY. The suit was continued after the death of Nathaniel GIST and was decided in favor of Elizabeth McCUNE and her husband by the KY Court. Later the suit was carried to the U.S. Supreme Court by the heir
2 CONC s of Nathaniel GIST, where the decision was reversed in their favor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1e) [1794]: Thomas GIST;&apos;s heirs took legal action against Isa
2 CONC ac MEASON [Besson?], and he paid them 1200 pounds for the entire property he had purchased at auction of more than 1,000 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1e) 1794
2 CONC , 5 Apr: Nathaniel GIST and Anne GIST sold Thomas GIST&apos;s remaining tracts of land which were their share from Thomas GIST&apos;s estate to Isaac MEASON for 800 pounds.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;
2 CONC font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1t) 1814, 27 Jan: Will brought into court in Bourbon Co., KY for final settlement of his property in KY.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;(1x) 1815, 15 Nov: Jesse BLEDSOE and Sarah his wife, Joseph BOSWELL and Judith his wife, Francis P. BLAIR and Eliza V. his wife, Ann E. HART widow late of Nath. HART deceased, Maria C. GIST, Henry C. GIST, and Thomas N. GIST, all heirs at la
2 CONC w of Nathaniel GIST, deceased, who was heir at law of Thomas GIST, deceased, all of the state of KY, sold to James ROGERS of Fayette Co., PA, the military warrant granted Thomas GIST 5 Jan 1780, Bourbon Co., KY, containing 1515 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(1c) 1865, 15 Feb: Fayette Co., PA deed of Mary MEASON of Fayette Co., PA, widow of Isaac MEASON, deceased, and Isaac BEESON of Mount Braddock, Fayett
2 CONC e Co., that whereas Christopher GIST agent of the (Virginia) Ohio Company and the guide of George WASHINGTON in his first tour across the Allegheny Mountains on his embassy from the Governor of Virginia to the French commandant on the Allegheny mad
2 CONC e what is believed to be the first English settlement west of the said mountains in the year 1753 upon the lands hereafter described then believed to be within the British Dominion of Virginia and continued to claim and hold the same - his rights t
2 CONC hereto becoming transferred to and recognized in his sons Thomas GIST Esquire, General Nathaniel GIST, and Richard GIST and for which lawful titles were acquired...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14pt;&q
2 CONC uot;&gt;[Besson could be related somehow through the Cromwell family of Baltimore County who married Bessons. Christopher Gist the Immigrant married Edith CROMWELL. Her only child, Richard Gist&amp;mdash;Thomas Gist&amp;rsquo;s grandfather&amp;mdas
2 CONC h;was raised by her brother, William Cromwell. William married Ann Besson 26 October, 1697. Md Historical Magazine, XIV, March 1919]&lt;span class=&quot;Apple-converted-space&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p
2 CONC &gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
