1 _DSCR information taken from Frederick J Hill&#39;s book &quot; A Half Step Behind the Pioneers
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Maren, Jens and their two sons Hans Peter Jensen and Rasmus Jensen&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;departed from Copenhagen&amp;nbsp;on May 28th 1866 on the steamship Aurora.&amp;nbsp;They then joined 201 persons from thei
2 CONC r company&amp;nbsp; in Hamberg and left on the sailing ship Cavouron June&amp;nbsp; 1 1866&amp;nbsp; The ship was overloaded and under provisioned&amp;nbsp; for the nine week trip. The water ration sank to one quart per familly per day at one point
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; Food and water were of poor quality and sickness broke out&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp; They reached New York Harbor on July 31. Their emigration agent was unable to reach agreement with any rial carrier to transpor
2 CONC t the immigrants directly west from New&amp;nbsp;York&amp;nbsp;They proceeded up the Hudson and on to Montreal, Canada because&amp;nbsp;this was a more economical route. They traveled by wood-fired steamboat up the St Lawrence River and then by rai
2 CONC l along the north shore of Lake Ontario and Lake Erie in freight or cattle cars. Cholera broke out.&amp;nbsp; Next the company was ferried across the St Clair River to Detroit and from there by rail through Chicago to Quincey, Illinois Then they to
2 CONC ok a journey across Northern Missouri&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The sick and dying were &amp;nbsp;not allowed&amp;nbsp;on the riverboat at St Joseph for the two day trip up the Missouri River to Wyoming, Nebraska.&amp;nbsp; Finally they left Wyoming fo
2 CONC r Salt Lake City on August 13th.&amp;nbsp; Maren became one of those sick with cholera&amp;nbsp; and died on August&amp;nbsp; 19 1866. She was buried somewhere along the Platte not far from Fort Kearney.&amp;nbsp; Over 100 persons in this company d
2 CONC ied &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Provisions were running short and they encountered a major snowstorm&amp;nbsp; in southeastern Wyoming as it neared&amp;nbsp; Fort Bridger.&amp;nbsp; Here they received provisions&amp;nbsp;from a relief train&amp;nbsp; an
2 CONC d a day or two later they were in Utah. On October 22 1866 they drove into Eighth Ward Square at the spot currently occupied by the Salt Lake&amp;nbsp; County Courthouse.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
