2 TITL Notes from Find a Grave on Col Nathaniel Gist 1733-1796
1 _OID d6195576-55e1-412a-8681-8946cd93ec24
1 DATE 1733-1796
1 PLAC Virginia, USA
1 _DSCR Virginia, Kentucky, Maryland
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;***** RESEARCHING *****&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;COL Nathaniel Gist&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;Veteran&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Birth: 15 Oct 1733 Baltimore City, Maryland, USA&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&g
2 CONC t;Death: 1796 (aged 62&amp;ndash;63) Clark County, Kentucky, USA&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Burial: Gist Family CemeteryClark County, Kentucky, USA&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Memorial #: 244936236&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Bio: Nathani
2 CONC el was the son of Christopher Gist.&amp;sim;Nathaniel Gist, A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of COLONEL. (1733&amp;ndash;1796). Continental officer. Virginia. Often mistaken for his uncle Nathaniel, this Gist (pronoun
2 CONC ced &quot;guest&quot;) was the son of the famous colonial scout Christopher Gist and first cousin of General Mordecai Gist. He took command of one of the sixteen Additional Continental Regiments on 11 January 1777.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Th
2 CONC e younger Nathaniel Gist lived among the Cherokee as an Indian trader from the mid-1750s until 1775 and was a hunting companion of Daniel Boone. Many scholars maintain that he was the father of Sequoyah (born 1760 or 1761). Gist, who served as a ca
2 CONC ptain of Virginia militia during the Seven Years&apos; War, attempted to persuade the Cherokee to remain neutral during the Revolution, as he also had doubts as to which side to take.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;By 1776 he had definitely taken t
2 CONC he Patriot side and was made a colonel in the Continental Army on 11 January 1777. Washington immediately pressed him into service to negotiate a peace with the Cherokee. By the end of the year, Gist was attempting to persuade Washington to make be
2 CONC tter use of the Patriots&apos; Indian allies, without much success.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Commanding Red Stone Fort in Pennsylvania in 1779, he was sent to reinforce Charleston, becoming a prisoner of war on 12 May 1780. He retired 1 Janua
2 CONC ry 1783. In 1793 he moved to his grant of seven thousand acres in Kentucky (awarded by Congress for his services during the Revolution) and died there on his Canewood plantation in 1796.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;His widow, Judith Cary Bell Gi
2 CONC st, married General Charles Scott, who served as governor of Kentucky from 1808 to 1819.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;b. October 15, 1733, Baltimore County, Maryland&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;d. 1796
2 CONC , Canewood, Clark County, Kentucky&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;parents: Christopher Gist &amp;amp; Sarah Howard&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&quot;served as colonel, Virginia Continental Line, 1777; was taken prisoner at Charleston, and retire
2 CONC , 1781&quot; - DAR lineage book, vol. 154, page 151.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Family:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Marriage 1 Wut-teh (Wurteh) Paint Clan (Bird Clan) b: ABT 1744&lt;/p&gt;</line><lin
2 CONC e>&lt;p&gt;Children:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;George &quot;Sequoyah&quot; Gist (Guess) b: ABT 1775 in Tuskegee,, Cherokee Nation&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Marriage 2: Judith Cary Bell b: 1750 in Virginia, Married: ABT 1783 in Buckingha
2 CONC m Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Children:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Sarah Howard Gist b: ABT 1784/1785 in , Buckingham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Henry Cary Gist b: ABT 1786 in , Buckingham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&
2 CONC gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Judith Bell Gist b: ABT 1788 in , Buckingham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Thomas Nathaniel Gist b: ABT 1790 in , Buckingham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Anna Maria Gist b: ABT 1791 in , Bucki
2 CONC ngham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Davidella C. (Dandella) Gist b: ABT 1793 in , Buckingham Co., Virginia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Elizabeth Violet H.&quot;Eliza&quot; Gist b: 1793/1795&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Maria Cec
2 CONC il Gist b: 18 Jul 1797 in Canewood, Bourbon Co., Kentucky&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;NOTES:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Info added per DARs &quot;Lineage Book of the Charter Members&quot; by Mary S L
2 CONC ockwood and published 1895 stating he was &quot;a colonel and grigadier of the Virginia Line&quot;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Sequoyah&apos;s father was either white or mixed-race white and Cherokee. Sources differ as to the exact identity of S
2 CONC equoyah&apos;s father, but many (including Mooney) suggested that he was possibly a fur trader, who would have been a man of some social status and financial backing. Based on available documentation, for decades historians believed that Sequoyah&a
2 CONC pos;s father was Nathaniel Gist, a commissioned officer with the Continental Army associated with George Washington.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Allegiance - United States, Service/branch - Infantry, Years of service - 1755&amp;ndash;1760 and 17
2 CONC 77&amp;ndash;1783. Rank - Colonel. Battles/wars fought in - Braddock&apos;s Expedition (1755), Forbes Expedition (1758), Cherokee War (1760), Battle of Paulus Hook (1779), and Siege of Charleston (1780).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Brief Biograp
2 CONC hy:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Nathaniel Gist (15 October 1733 &amp;ndash; 1812) was born in Maryland and fought during the French and Indian War and the American Revolutionary War. He was reputed to be the father of Sequoyah the famous Cheroke
2 CONC e by Wut-teh. Like his father Christopher Gist (1706&amp;ndash;1759), Nathaniel became a frontiersman and he once briefly traveled with Daniel Boone. He served in Braddock&apos;s Expedition in 1755 and the Forbes Expedition in 1758. The outbreak o
2 CONC f the American Revolution found him on the frontier. At first suspected of sympathizing with the British, he convinced the Americans of his loyalty.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;George Washington, a close friend of his father, authorized him to f
2 CONC orm Gist&apos;s Additional Continental Regiment in January 1777. Gist probably participated in Light Horse Harry Lee&apos;s Paulus Hook Raid in 1779. He and his regiment were captured at the Siege of Charleston in May 1780. After the war, he took a
2 CONC n American wife Judith Cary Bell (1750&amp;ndash;1833) and the couple had four daughters, one of whom married Francis P. Blair. He is variously said to have died in 1796, 1812, or at the end of the War of 1812. He is confused with his uncle Nathani
2 CONC el Gist (1707&amp;ndash;1780). He was a first cousin of Mordecai Gist.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Lineage Book - National Society of the Daughters of the American ..., Volume 36 &apos;Daughters of the American Revolution - 1912 http://books.goo
2 CONC gle.com/books?id=mmUZAQAAIAAJ&amp;amp;pg=PA147&amp;amp;img=1&amp;amp;zoom=3&amp;amp;hl=en&amp;amp;sig=ACfU3U2JDMpQ-izsNKrT7m-b3lT2sTOEMA&amp;amp;ci=81%2C628%2C768%2C380&amp;amp;edge=0&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Nathaniel was the son of Captai
2 CONC n Christopher Gist[, himself famous as an explorer of the West in 1750 and as guide to the young George Washington in the Ohio River Region. His personal journals are considered the bedrock of history of the West for the earliest emigrant times.&lt
2 CONC ;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Nathaniel had a brilliant career in the southeast Indian Nation; trading in 1750&apos;s, later as Lieutenant to Major George Washington in 1755. Due to his intimate negotiations with the Stuarts, agents, and Old Hop, an
2 CONC d other chiefs, he was suspected of being a double agent in the Revolutionary War Years, 1770&apos;s and came close to hanging; but Gist was diplomatically reunited with Geo. Washington who elevated him to Colonel of a Continental regiment. He reti
2 CONC red in January, 1781.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;MOST NOTABLE:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Sequoyah George Gist Guess (b. 1770, d. 1843)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Son of Col. Nathaniel Gist and Wur-Teh Watts&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><l
